| =============================================================================
|  solution-splunk-queries.spl — Complete Annotated SPL Solutions
|  Week 8, Friday: Splunk Observability Lab
|  Reference: do not open until you have attempted each query yourself.
| =============================================================================


| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| SOLUTION 1 — Requests Per Minute
|
| What it answers: How many log events arrived each minute over the last hour?
|
| Pipeline breakdown:
|   [1] index=main sourcetype=_json earliest=-1h
|       Base search — retrieve all events from the main index with our
|       sourcetype, scoped to the last 60 minutes. earliest=-1h is a
|       relative time modifier; Splunk resolves it to a Unix timestamp at
|       search execution time.
|
|   [2] | timechart span=1m count as "Requests"
|       timechart bins events into equal-width time buckets (span=1m = 1 minute
|       each) and counts how many events fall into each bucket.
|       The result is a table with _time as the X axis and Requests as Y.
|       Visualize this as a Line Chart to see traffic patterns over time.
| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=main sourcetype=_json earliest=-1h
| timechart span=1m count as "Requests"


| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| SOLUTION 2 — Error Rate Percentage
|
| What it answers: What percentage of requests in the last hour were errors?
|
| Pipeline breakdown:
|   [1] index=main sourcetype=_json earliest=-1h
|       Retrieve all events — not just errors — so we have a denominator.
|
|   [2] | eval is_error=if(level="ERROR" OR status_code>=500, 1, 0)
|       Create a binary field: 1 for error events, 0 for healthy events.
|       OR combines two conditions — catches both logged ERROR level events
|       and HTTP 5xx responses that might have been logged at WARN level.
|
|   [3] | stats sum(is_error) as errors, count as total
|       Single-pass aggregation:
|         - sum(is_error) adds up all the 1s to get the error count
|         - count gives total events (our denominator)
|
|   [4] | eval error_pct=round(errors / total * 100, 2)
|       Compute the percentage. round(..., 2) keeps two decimal places.
|       Formula: errors / total * 100 = percentage of requests that errored.
|
|   [5] | table total, errors, error_pct
|       Present only the three meaningful columns.
| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=main sourcetype=_json earliest=-1h
| eval is_error=if(level="ERROR" OR status_code>=500, 1, 0)
| stats sum(is_error) as errors, count as total
| eval error_pct=round(errors / total * 100, 2)
| table total, errors, error_pct


| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| SOLUTION 3 — Response Time Distribution Per Endpoint
|
| What it answers: For each endpoint, what are the avg / p50 / p95 / p99 / max
|                  response times in milliseconds?
|
| Pipeline breakdown:
|   [1] index=main sourcetype=_json duration_ms=* earliest=-1h
|       duration_ms=* filters to only events where duration_ms is present
|       (not null/missing). Without this filter, non-request events like
|       startup banners would pull down the percentile calculations.
|
|   [2] | stats avg(duration_ms) as avg_ms,
|               perc50(duration_ms) as p50_ms,
|               perc95(duration_ms) as p95_ms,
|               perc99(duration_ms) as p99_ms,
|               max(duration_ms) as max_ms
|         by endpoint
|       Single stats command computes all five percentiles simultaneously,
|       grouped by the endpoint field. Each row is one unique endpoint.
|       perc50 = median. The gap between p95 and p99 reveals tail latency.
|
|   [3] | eval avg_ms=round(avg_ms, 2)
|       Round the average to 2 decimal places for readability.
|       Percentile functions return integers so they do not need rounding.
|
|   [4] | sort -avg_ms
|       Sort descending by average so the slowest endpoints are at the top.
|       The minus sign (-) means descending in SPL sort syntax.
| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=main sourcetype=_json duration_ms=* earliest=-1h
| stats avg(duration_ms) as avg_ms,
        perc50(duration_ms) as p50_ms,
        perc95(duration_ms) as p95_ms,
        perc99(duration_ms) as p99_ms,
        max(duration_ms) as max_ms
  by endpoint
| eval avg_ms=round(avg_ms, 2)
| sort -avg_ms


| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| SOLUTION 4 — Top Endpoints by Volume
|
| What it answers: Which endpoints receive the most traffic?
|
| Pipeline breakdown:
|   [1] index=main sourcetype=_json earliest=-1h
|       All events — not filtered by error — to see total traffic per path.
|
|   [2] | stats count as request_count by endpoint
|       Count events grouped by the endpoint MDC field.
|       Each unique URI becomes one row.
|
|   [3] | sort -request_count
|       Sort descending so the busiest endpoint is row 1.
|
|   [4] | head 10
|       Limit to the top 10 results. In a dashboard panel, head 10
|       ensures the table does not grow unbounded as new endpoints appear.
| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=main sourcetype=_json earliest=-1h
| stats count as request_count by endpoint
| sort -request_count
| head 10


| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| SOLUTION 5 — Alert Threshold Check
|
| What it answers: Did more than 10 errors occur in the last 5 minutes?
|                  (Returns a row only when the answer is yes.)
|
| Why this pattern works for Splunk alerts:
|   Splunk alerts are configured to fire when the search returns N results.
|   By setting trigger condition to "Number of Results > 0" and writing a
|   search that returns 0 rows when everything is fine and 1 row when the
|   threshold is exceeded, the alert becomes self-documenting: a result row
|   means "something is wrong", no rows means "all clear".
|
| Pipeline breakdown:
|   [1] index=main sourcetype=_json level=ERROR earliest=-5m
|       Narrow the search to error events in the last 5 minutes only.
|       Using earliest=-5m instead of -1h keeps the scheduled search fast —
|       each evaluation window only scans a small slice of data.
|
|   [2] | stats count as error_count
|       Collapse all matching events into a single row with the total count.
|       If 0 errors occurred, this returns one row with error_count=0.
|
|   [3] | where error_count > 10
|       Filter out the "all clear" row. When error_count is 10 or below,
|       where removes the row and the search returns 0 results — alert stays
|       silent. When error_count exceeds 10, the row survives — alert fires.
|
| Tuning advice:
|   - In high-traffic systems replace count with a rate: errors/total*100
|     and alert when error_pct > 1 (1%) rather than a raw count of 10.
|   - Adjust earliest=-5m to match your alert schedule interval.
|   - Add a by endpoint clause if you want per-endpoint alerts.
| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=main sourcetype=_json level=ERROR earliest=-5m
| stats count as error_count
| where error_count > 10


| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| BONUS — 5xx Error Search with Endpoint Breakdown (Part 3, Search 3)
|
| What it answers: How many 5xx errors did each endpoint generate?
|
| Pipeline breakdown:
|   [1] index=main sourcetype=_json level=ERROR earliest=-1h
|       Filter to ERROR-level events only.
|
|   [2] | stats count as error_count by endpoint
|       Count errors grouped by endpoint URI.
|
|   [3] | sort -error_count
|       Worst offenders at the top.
| -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

index=main sourcetype=_json level=ERROR earliest=-1h
| stats count as error_count by endpoint
| sort -error_count
